^SAMPLE = GSM9294007
!Sample_title = PBMC Ctrl Sham
!Sample_geo_accession = GSM9294007
!Sample_status = Public on Mar 26 2026
!Sample_submission_date = Nov 18 2025
!Sample_last_update_date = Mar 26 2026
!Sample_type = SRA
!Sample_channel_count = 1
!Sample_source_name_ch1 = PBMC
!Sample_organism_ch1 = Mus musculus
!Sample_taxid_ch1 = 10090
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = tissue: PBMC
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = strain: C57BL/6N
!Sample_characteristics_ch1 = treatment: Sham Surgery; control diet for 6 weeks
!Sample_treatment_protocol_ch1 = Mice had ad libitum access to water and a 60% kcal fat diet (no. d12492; Research Diets, Inc., New Brunswick, NJ) or a normal 18% kcal fat diet (no. 2018SX; Harlan Laboratories, Indianapolis, IN) for 6 weeks before undergoing dMCAO or sham surgery.
!Sample_molecule_ch1 = total RNA
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = Brain tissue was dissociated with the Miltenyi neural dissociation kit following the manufacturer’s instructions, with minor adjustments to optimize the isolation of endothelial and immune cells. In brief, 4–5 mice per treatment group were anesthetized with ketamine/xylazine solution and perfused with 15mL ice cold phosphate buffered saline (PBS) following cardiac blood collection. The stroke core and peri-infarct cortex, defined as ~2mm surrounding the visible lesion, were dissected and pooled by condition, minced, and digested using the neural dissociation kit (Miltenyi, 130–092-628). Brain homogenates were further diluted in Dulbecco’s PBS (dPBS) and filtered through a 100 µm strainer. The filtered homogenate was then centrifuged (300 g for 10 minutes) and the pellets were resuspended in 0.9 M sucrose in dPBS followed by centrifugation for 20 minutes at 850 g at 4 °C to separate the myelin. Cell pellets were resuspended in FACS buffer (1% BSA in PBS with 2 mM EDTA) and blocked for 5 min on ice with Fc block (CD16/CD32, BD 553141), followed by staining for 10 minutes with anti-CD31-PE/TR594 (1:200, BD 563616) and anti-CD45-PE/Cy7 (1:200, BD 103114) antibodies. Dead cells were excluded by staining with SYTOX Blue Dead Cell Stain (1:5,000, Thermo Fisher Scientific S34857).
!Sample_extract_protocol_ch1 = Reagents of the Chromium Single Cell 3’ GEM & Gel Bead Kit v3.1 (10X Genomics, 1000121) were thawed and prepared according to the manufacturer’s protocol. Cells and master mix solution were adjusted to target 10,000 cells per sample and loaded on a standard Chromium Controller (10X Genomics, 1000204) according to manufacturer protocols. We applied 11 PCR cycles to generate cDNA. Library construction was conducted using the Chromium Single Cell 3’ Library Construction Kit v3 (10X Genomics, 1000121). All reaction and quality control steps were carried out according to the manufacturer’s protocol and with recommended reagents, consumables, and instruments. Quality control of cDNA and libraries was conducted using a Bioanalyzer (Agilent) at the Stanford Protein and Nucleic Acid Facility. Illumina sequencing of the resulting libraries was performed by Novogene (https://en.novogene.com/) on an Illumina NovaSeq S4 (Illumina). Base calling, demultiplexing, and generation of FastQ files were conducted by Novogene.
!Sample_description = Library name: K_01
!Sample_description = 10x Genomics
!Sample_data_processing = The Cell Ranger (v.6.0.0) analysis pipeline was employed to align sequencing reads to the mm10-GRCh38 hybrid reference genome and quantify barcodes and unique molecular identifiers (UMIs). Reads mapping to introns were excluded. Cells with a high mitochondrial content (>20%) relative to endogenous RNAs were removed in Seurat. Cells were also removed if they expressed fewer than 100 unique genes. After filtering, the following cell counts remained: blood: 7,486 (control diet sham), 5,722 (control diet stroke), 7,519 (high-fat diet sham), and 6,710 (high-fat diet stroke); brain: 5,477 (control diet sham), 11,068 (control diet stroke), 5,236 (high-fat diet sham), and 12,026 (high-fat diet stroke). Genes not detected in any cell were removed from subsequent analysis.
!Sample_data_processing = Mitochondrial and cell cycle gene regression was performed to ensure that clustering reflects true cell type identities. Principal component analysis was performed on the 2000 most variable genes of the dataset followed by UMAP clustering using the Seurat R Package (v. 4.3.0.1) on the first 20 principal components.
!Sample_data_processing = Identification of significant clusters was performed via the standard FindClusters()algorithm in the Seurat package with a resolution parameter of 0.4 for PBMCs and 0.6 for brain. The cell type of each significant cluster was identified using marker genes generated by the Seurat function FindAllMarkers(). Major cell types were identified using formerly established cell markers in the literature (McManaman et al. 2013, Cho et al. 2017; Qian et al. 2017, Tillander et al. 2017; Heden et al. 2023, (Gropler et al. 2009; van Harmelen et al. 2007; Chen et al. 2015; VandeKopple et al. 2019; Maier et al. 2017; van Dierendonck et al. 2020; de la Rosa Rodriguez et al. 2021; Pulido et al. 2011; Dejgaard and Presley 2019; Zhang et al. 2024; Kiss and Nilsson 2014; Chen and Farese 2005; Chitraju et al. 2019; Chen 2006; Chitraju et al. 2017; Castoldi et al. 2020; Yen et al. 2008; Uchańska et al. 2024, CellMarker.2.0). One PBMC cluster was removed as identified contaminating red blood cells and three brain clusters were removed as unidentifiable clusters. The UMAPs within each cluster and condition were generated using ggplot2 (v. 3.4.4). Cell counts were found once the clusters were subset by condition.
!Sample_data_processing = Assembly: mm10
!Sample_data_processing = Supplementary files format and content: Tab-separated values files and matrix files
